    narrative: 'Pais is the only person in the open record who has put a description of a craft with the reported UAP flight characteristics into the United States patent system, with the United States Navy as assignee and his own name on the front. That is the entire reason he belongs on this board, and it is a documentary fact independent of whether anything he described works.  Between July 2015 and March 2018 he filed five applications from NAWCAD at NAS Patuxent River, every one of them assigned to \'The United States of America as represented by the Secretary of the Navy\': an electromagnetic field generator, a craft using an inertial mass reduction device, a high frequency gravitational wave generator, a piezoelectricity-induced room temperature superconductor, and a plasma compression fusion device. Three were granted. Two published as applications.  The Navy\'s own invention-disclosure briefing for the inertial mass reduction device, Navy Case PAX 205, was released under FOIA and is the most useful document in the set because Pais wrote it for an internal audience rather than for an examiner. It describes a \'hybrid aerospace / undersea craft (HAUC)\' that would \'move with great ease through the air / space / water mediums\' enclosed in a \'Vacuum plasma bubble/sheath\', achieving \'extreme aerial and underwater speeds (lack of air/water-skin friction) and enhanced stealth capabilities (non-linear scattering of RF and sonar signals)\'. Set that description beside the flight characteristics the Navy\'s own aviators reported in the same decade and the overlap is not subtle: transmedium travel, extreme acceleration, and low observability, itemised in a Navy invention disclosure.  The same briefing is equally explicit about what did not exist. Pais states, twice, that this \'is a theoretical concept - no prototype in existence, as yet\', gives the invention original date as 6 March 2015, and proposes Section 219 NISE funding of 250,000 dollars in labour and 250,000 dollars in NAWCAD facility cost to investigate it experimentally. A slide header reads \'EXPERIMENT TRUMPS THEORY EVERYTIME!\'. Whatever else the documents are, on their face they are a proposal to test an idea, written by someone asking for money to test it.  That is where the record turns against the strong reading. NAWCAD did fund the work, and reporting based on documents released under FOIA says it spent roughly half a million dollars between 2016 and 2019 and concluded that the effect could not be demonstrated, with no further research conducted. The figure matches the two 250,000 dollar lines Pais proposed. A board that leaves that out is not making the case stronger, it is making it dismissible.  What remains genuinely unusual, and is not disposed of by the negative test result, is the institutional behaviour around the filings. A patent examiner\'s job is to reject a claim that is not enabled, and the record as reported is that one was rejected on exactly that ground and then granted after the Naval Aviation Enterprise\'s chief technology officer wrote to the USPTO asserting operability and warning that China was pursuing the same capability. Agencies do not usually spend senior technical credibility forcing through a patent they believe describes nothing. Either the Navy believed the physics, or it wanted the priority date on the public record for reasons it has not stated. Both readings are live, and neither is established.',
